# workflow-conventions.allow — the two graded tables behind the four fleet
# workflow conventions verify_workflow_conventions.py asserts. Both are keyed on
# the repository the gate resolved from its `origin` remote, because this one
# file is shared by every consumer through the submodule; rows for other
# repositories are read, checked for typos, and otherwise left alone.
# docs/code-quality-tooling.md#four-fleet-workflow-conventions-workflow-lint
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. THE CENSUS RATCHET: CENSUS | <repo> | <check> | <count> | <reason>
#
#   The frozen number of unexcused findings that RAMPING check has in that
#   repository. It may only go down. Above it the gate FAILS whatever
#   WORKFLOW_CONVENTIONS_GATE says, and a check with findings and no row FAILS
#   too — so a new violation is red on the day it lands even while its
#   convention is still ramping, which is the half a report-and-pass ramp does
#   not have on its own. An ARMED check counts zero here, because its findings
#   already failed on their own: arming a check retires its row, and one finding
#   never produces two verdicts.
#   Below it: in ANTfrastructure, whose commit can edit this file beside the fix, an
#   unrecorded shrink FAILS like every other allow file here. In a consumer,
#   which reads this table through the submodule and cannot edit it, the shrink
#   is printed with the number to write down and passes — that asymmetry is why
#   this is a count and not the frozen offender list shellcheck-warnings.allow
#   can afford.
#   These numbers are measured, never typed: they are the output of the gate
#   itself over each repository's default branch, and nothing else in the tree
#   restates them. The measurement below is 2026-09-09, all eight repositories at
#   their `origin` head, 49 files (37 workflows + 12 composite actions).
#
CENSUS | ANTfrastructure | job-timeout | 1 | build-test-python-package-on-windows in python-ci-windows.yml — the one job in this repo still without a bound. Clear it and this check can be armed here beside `permissions`, and this row deleted
CENSUS | ANTfrastructure | artifact-error | 2 | The two upload sites in python-ci-linux.yml and python-ci-windows.yml. Both are diagnostic uploads whose emptiness means an upstream failure; whether that is an excuse or a fix is the owner's call, and until it is made the count may not grow
CENSUS | BeschleunigerBallett | job-timeout | 12 | 11 in reusable-linux.yml (Linux.yml until 2026-09-24) plus windows-x64.yml's pester-tests; its build job calls the hub's container-ci-windows.yml since 2026-09-26, which bounds itself. reusable-linux.yml's own comments price two lanes at ~25 minutes each and the run at 2h13m, then warn about the 6-hour cap every one of these jobs sits under
CENSUS | BeschleunigerBallett | permissions | 11 | Every job in reusable-linux.yml (was Linux.yml); windows-x64.yml declares one at the top level. One `permissions:` block at the top of reusable-linux.yml clears all twelve at once
CENSUS | BeschleunigerBallett | artifact-error | 1 | The package upload in reusable-linux.yml, an artifact a release actually ships, which is where an empty upload is worst. windows-x64.yml's moved to the hub's container-ci-windows.yml on 2026-09-26, whose upload fails on an empty product
CENSUS | OrchestrANT | permissions | 2 | The `linux` and `windows` jobs, which call the hub's reusable python-ci workflows; the caller declares the token scope, so it cannot be fixed in the callee
#
# `runner-ban` has no row anywhere, and never will: it is armed with no knob, so
# its ceiling is zero and a single `*-latest` label is a hard failure the day it
# is written. It is measured at 0 across all eight repositories today.
# `ANTfrastructure | permissions` has no row for the same reason — preflight.sh
# arms `permissions` for this repo, which is measured clean of it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. THE EXCUSED-WITH-REASON TABLE: <repo> | <path> | <check> | <detail> | <reason>
#
#   <repo>    the repository the row is about, as .github/consumers.json declares
#             it. A repo name consumers.json does not declare is a typo and FAILS.
#   <path>    the workflow or action file, relative to that repository's root.
#   <check>   runner-ban | job-timeout | permissions | artifact-error
#   <detail>  the job id (job-timeout, permissions), the offending runner label
#             (runner-ban), or the upload step's `name:` (artifact-error).
#   <reason>  why the deviation is correct. The rest of the line, `|` allowed.
#
# Same contract as verify_ci_image_refs.py's EXCUSED dict, which this replaces
# for these four checks: a deliberate deviation is DECLARED, never silent, and a
# row that matches no live finding is STALE and fails — so the list can only
# shrink by becoming true. A stale row fails whatever the arming says; the
# arming knob ramps the FINDINGS, never the bookkeeping. Excusing a finding also
# lowers the census count above it, so the two tables have to agree.
#
# EMPTY ON PURPOSE, 2026-09-09. Not one of the 68 findings the census freezes is
# excused here: excusing a finding to make a lane green is the opposite of the
# job, and every one of them is a decision for the repository's owner.
#
# Worked example of the row shape, for whoever adds the first one:
#
#   ANTfrastructure | .github/workflows/python-ci-windows.yml | artifact-error | Upload files, logs & Zip | The workflow header states the choice: an empty dist/ here means the build already failed upstream, and `error` would fail the job a second time for the same cause while losing the logs/ and docs/test_results/ the artifact carries for the diagnosis.
